+            // InvalidOperationException ("... likely due to a transient failure ..."), so the real
+            // ADO.NET exception can sit two wrappers deep: InvalidOperationException ->
+            // DbUpdateException -> the provider's own DbException. DbUpdateException does not itself
+            // derive from DbException, so an unwrap that only checks a fixed number of
+            // InnerException hops can miss that middle layer and land on null. Walk the whole
